From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] a window the reader can see through may not take the navigation bar

0053 repaired the navigation bar that never came back after a dialog left
immersive mode. This is the other half of the same report: the solid black band
that stands where the bar was for as long as the dialog is up.

Measured on V84, from screencap of the framebuffer and dumpsys window:

    band                    y=1358, 90 rows
    NavigationBar mFrame    [0,1358][1072,1448]
    band grey               33, the blackest value in the same frame
    main window Cur insets  content=[0,45][0,90]
    policy                  mContent=(0,0)-(1072,1448)

The main window is still reserving 90 px for a bar the policy reserves nothing
for. It carries FLAG_DRAWS_SYSTEM_BAR_BACKGROUNDS and
SYSTEM_UI_FLAG_LAYOUT_HIDE_NAVIGATION, so it is laid out over those 90 px and
its decor fills them with navigationBarColor. Put that same window into
immersive on its own instead -- `settings put global policy_control
immersive.full=com.tencent.weread.eink` -- and its insets become [0,0][0,0] and
it paints its page to the bottom edge, white, no band. The band is a decoration
drawn for a bar that is not there, not a surface that failed to draw.

The colour is AOSP's, not the application's. themes_material.xml defaults
navigationBarColor to @color/black in Theme.Material and Theme.Material.Light
alike, and WeRead's own resource table sets navigationBarColor, statusBarColor
and windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds nowhere at all -- zero occurrences of
0x01010457, 0x01010456 and 0x01010455. Every application on a stock theme
paints that black: unseen under an opaque white navigation bar, revealed the
moment something takes the bar away. Repainting it white would hide one
instance of a mechanism that will come back.

The first form of this patch went the other way and refused the hiding
altogether -- canHideNavigationBar() returning false, which AOSP provides for
exactly that purpose. That is wrong on this device, and the reading view is
why. com.tencent.weread.ReaderFragmentActivity.onResume() is four calls:

    getWindow()
    changeToFullScreen(window)
    AccountSettingManager.setLastWakeTime(currentTimeMillis())
    super.onResume()

and changeToFullScreen delegates to FullScreenAction$DefaultImpls, which is

    const/16 v1, 0x2000                 LIGHT_STATUS_BAR, retained
    const/16 v0, 0x1706                 IMMERSIVE_STICKY | HIDE_NAVIGATION
                                        | FULLSCREEN | the three LAYOUT_ flags
    QMUIStatusBarHelper.retainSystemUiFlag(window, v0, v1)
    decorView.setSystemUiVisibility(result)
    window.addFlags(FLAG_TRANSLUCENT_STATUS)

-- the same 0x1706 measured on the dialog, out of the same helper. Reading a
book is full screen on every resume, with no setting behind it. Refusing that
would take 90 px of 1448 from the one thing this device exists to do, 6.2% of
every page, and park Back, Home and Recents under every page of every book.

What the band needs is narrower: a window may hide the bar only if it covers
what it takes. An application window whose format is not OPAQUE does not -- the
reader is looking through it at a window that still has the inset, and that
window behind is the one painting the black. WeRead's dialog is exactly that
(TYPE_APPLICATION, translucent, FLAG_DIM_BEHIND, asking for IMMERSIVE_STICKY),
and the reading view is not: its AppTheme sets no windowIsTranslucent, and the
four styles in the APK that do are AppTheme.Transparent, the captcha popup,
TTSTheme and the daemon's invisible activity.

So the dialog keeps the bar, and with it Back and Home -- which is also the
answer to the other half of what the reader reported, a bottom bar that
answered no tap. The reading view is untouched: it hides the bar, is relaid out
with no bottom inset, and paints those rows itself.

The test is confined to application windows so that the keyguard and the
status bar, which reach this code as the candidate window in their own cases,
keep deciding for themselves.

Left alone: SYSTEM_UI_FLAG_FULLSCREEN still hides the status bar, and the same
decoration exists at the top, statusBarColor defaulting to
?attr/colorPrimaryDark -- @color/material_grey_600 under a light theme. Nothing
on this device has been seen to expose it, because WeRead's dialog hides the
status bar too and the top 45 px stay white. Recorded in open-items rather than
changed.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/policy/PhoneWindowManager.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/policy/PhoneWindowManager.java
@@ -7906,6 +7906,20 @@
         int tmpVisibility = PolicyControl.getSystemUiVisibility(win, null)
                 & ~mResettingSystemUiFlags
                 & ~mForceClearedSystemUiFlags;
+        final int candidateType = win.getAttrs().type;
+        if (candidateType >= FIRST_APPLICATION_WINDOW && candidateType <= LAST_SUB_WINDOW
+                && win.getAttrs().format != PixelFormat.OPAQUE) {
+            // PW3: whoever hides the navigation bar takes its 90 px, and a window the
+            // reader can see through is not covering them. The window behind it was laid
+            // out against the stable inset and paints navigationBarColor in that strip --
+            // @color/black in every stock theme -- so the bar's place turns into a black
+            // band. WeRead's agreement dialog does exactly this: TYPE_APPLICATION,
+            // translucent, FLAG_DIM_BEHIND, asking for IMMERSIVE_STICKY. The reader also
+            // loses Back and Home while it is up, and this device has no key behind them.
+            // An opaque window is left alone: hiding the bar relays it out with no bottom
+            // inset and it paints the strip itself, which is what a reading view needs.
+            tmpVisibility &= ~View.SYSTEM_UI_FLAG_HIDE_NAVIGATION;
+        }
         if (mForcingShowNavBar && win.getSurfaceLayer() < mForcingShowNavBarLayer) {
             tmpVisibility &= ~PolicyControl.adjustClearableFlags(win, View.SYSTEM_UI_CLEARABLE_FLAGS);
         }
